Thanks @grinem  — both points match my reading.

**Gap 1:** Agreed that `claimResumeAttempt` should distinguish a lost CAS while the row is still waiting from a row that actually moved on. In [#44765](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/pull/44765) I only retried the process-local `ExecutionAlreadyResumingError`, so the cross-process version conflict still bubbles up as `local: false`. A distinct `version_conflict` outcome with `currentAttempts` is the right shape.

One caveat on the snippet: the post-update `findOne` is still a second race, so `UPDATE ... RETURNING` would classify in one statement where supported, with a fallback for SQLite. The retry also needs to be bounded and should not spin while another claim keeps winning.

**Gap 2:** Agreed. The parked-parent sweep is background maintenance and should not spend the operational resume budget. Reusing `resumeAttempts` makes `N8N_EXECUTIONS_MAX_RESUME_ATTEMPTS=1` unsafe. A dedicated lease is better; it must be released in `finally` and be recoverable if the process dies. `SKIP LOCKED` is Postgres-only, so a dedicated lease column/timestamp is the portable option.

I left both out of [#44765](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/pull/44765) to keep it scoped to the single-main race; this issue tracks them.
